{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app at 3405b31b27c6aa70e206e9ff69d2f51ef82f3b7d. The resident passes a queue bound to the kernel, so the shipped sequencer is not unbounded.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. ResidentQueueDepth is a named constant, 32, at app.go:61, passed as MaxQueueDepth at the kernel.NewSubmitter site at app.go:820. The bound counts the submission holding the sequencer lock, so 32 means one in progress and 31 waiting. Submitter.QueueDepth() exposes the configured bound so a test can assert the wiring rather than infer it from timing.\n\nThe bound belongs to Gitseq's resident, not to the kernel. A program embedding the kernel directly may leave MaxQueueDepth unset and get an unbounded queue; that is the embedding opt-out, not a posture Gitseq takes for itself.\n\nTESTS. TestResidentSequencerIsBounded reads workspace.submitter.QueueDepth() after forcing the submitter into existence, and fails with \"resident sequencer is unbounded; the queue bound was not passed to the kernel\". That wording matters: the failure names the wiring, which is the thing that can silently regress. The kernel can grow a perfect bound and the resident can still fail to pass it, and nothing else in the suite would notice.\n\nGATES, all at this commit, uncached: go vet clean, go build clean, ./internal/app/... 36.381s, ./internal/kernel/... 131.778s, -race on ./internal/kernel/... 94.192s.\n\nWHY THIS RESTS ONLY ON THE REQUEST AND THE PROMISE, which departs from what the request asked for and should be checked. The request asks replacement artifacts to cite the correct live predecessors. At this path there is no such citation available: every live artifact at the exact string internal/app is stale — 845e3cc9, 00c6eefb, 6a38030c, 82a33e46 and the v3 candidate 6b5e0b34. Citing any of them would make this artifact stale the moment it was recorded, which is the defect that has already cost two replacement artifacts in this repository.\n\nSo I have cited the request and the promise, both live and non-stale, and named the predecessor here in prose instead: 82a33e46 is the one a reader should follow back, and it is what the successor at this path must retire when this head merges. If the reviewer thinks a stale-at-birth artifact citing 82a33e46 is the better trade, that is a fair objection and I would rather be told than have it pass silently.\n\nThe kernel half of this change does not have this problem: its predecessor d97eed89 is live and not stale, and artifact 774efb29 cites it.\n\nWHY THIS COMMIT EXISTS. A re-cut of the same tree onto main 27603774 under request 48c709bd. The previous head a86f35e1 cited the retired f6c96085 in limits.md and conflicts with main there since the citation repair merged; see assert b0413456.","body":{"commit":"3405b31b27c6aa70e206e9ff69d2f51ef82f3b7d","path":"internal/app"}}